<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!--
      The one place the version number is written down.

      0.1.0 rather than 0.0.1: in semver a leading 0 already means "unstable,
      anything may change", so the whole 0.x range is the alpha and the patch
      digit is free to mean what it means everywhere else — a fix. Starting at
      0.0.1 would spend the minor digit on nothing and leave no room to say
      "this release added something" versus "this release fixed something".

      It is NOT bumped by CI. A number that moves on every build identifies
      nothing: "the bug is in 0.4.7" stops being a sentence once 0.4.7 was one
      of forty builds that afternoon. A release version moves when a person
      pushes a `v*` tag, and release.yml takes the number from that tag; an
      untagged bundle is stamped `<this>-alpha.<run number>` so it sorts before
      the release it precedes and can never be mistaken for it.

      Betas need no machinery of their own — tag `v0.3.0-beta.1` and semver
      already orders it before `v0.3.0`. 1.0.0 is the first release that
      promises the document format will not move under you, and it arrives in
      the same change that drops the alpha banner from README.md and the
      unconditional prerelease flag from release.yml.
    -->
    <VersionPrefix>0.1.0</VersionPrefix>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
